                    "comment": "scipy.correlate - how far one signal trails another"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1066,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Slides one signal past another and finds where they match best: how far one is behind the other - the lag, in seconds - and how strongly they match there.\n\nUse it to measure synchrony: whether a dancer leads or follows the music's pulse and by how much; how far a performer's movement trails another's (or a mirror's); the delay through a sensor, a network or a processing chain. A strength near 1 means the two move alike once the lag is allowed for; near 0, that they are unrelated.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.correlate <sample rate>\nDefault: sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first signal. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second signal, held: send it first.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so the lag comes out in seconds.\n\nmax lag (float box, also an inlet):\nOnly look for lags up to this many seconds either way. 0 (the default) looks at all of them. For a repeating signal several lags match; this keeps the search near zero.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlag:\nHow far a trails b, in seconds: positive when a comes later, negative when it comes earlier.\n\nstrength:\nHow well they match at that lag, from -1 to 1 (both are standardized first, so the size of the signals does not matter).\n\ncorrelation:\nThe match at every lag.\n\nlags:\nThe lag of each value of correlation, in seconds. Sent first, then correlation, strength and lag.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_pair subpatcher makes a wave (b) and the same wave 12 samples later (a). At 60 samples a second the lag comes out as 0.2 seconds, with a strength near 1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.correlate finds a short pattern within a signal. scipy.coherence measures how two signals share each rhythm. np.corrcoef measures how alike two signals are with no lag allowed.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1050,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Slides one signal past another and finds where they match best: how far one is behind the other - the lag, in seconds - and how strongly they match there.\n\nUse it to measure synchrony: whether a dancer leads or follows the music's pulse and by how much; how far a performer's movement trails another's (or a mirror's); the delay through a sensor, a network or a processing chain. A strength near 1 means the two move alike once the lag is allowed for; near 0, that they are unrelated.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.correlate <sample rate>\nDefault: sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first signal. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second signal, held: send it first.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so the lag comes out in seconds.\n\nmax lag (float box, also an inlet):\nOnly look for lags up to this many seconds either way. 0 (the default) looks at all of them. For a repeating signal several lags match; this keeps the search near zero.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlag:\nHow far a trails b, in seconds: positive when a comes later, negative when it comes earlier.\n\nstrength:\nHow well they match at that lag, from -1 to 1 (both are standardized first, so the size of the signals does not matter).\n\ncorrelation:\nThe match at every lag.\n\nlags:\nThe lag of each value of correlation, in seconds. Sent first, then correlation, strength and lag.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_pair subpatcher makes a wave (b) and the same wave 12 samples later (a). At 60 samples a second the lag comes out as 0.2 seconds, with a strength near 1.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.correlate finds a short pattern within a signal. scipy.coherence measures how two signals share each rhythm. np.corrcoef measures how alike two signals are with no lag allowed."
                },
